#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85183c94-e141-50ef-945b-894dbe2fe6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203511} {:answer/id #uuid "99928875-8585-539b-a856-da07dd6fd1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203512} {:answer/id #uuid "df332339-0c54-528c-9ada-ee89985519d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203513} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2d8ffc-a69d-5fdb-b341-6e99f1e6830b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203514} {:answer/id #uuid "75aac1bc-0989-5fdf-a037-6f51b15ba95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203515}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0b4d3b-494b-55d4-9523-196ee64019a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203510}] :question/id #uuid "6bda3cab-2f65-5bfd-a054-8baee9c84dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288058" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n1. $4x - 2 = 4x + 3$\n2. $3x + 4 = 3x + 4$\n3. $5x = 2x + 6$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62ec00bb-a7dc-5ae8-ae64-a716f57687a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102106" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-1$\nII. - $x=2$\nIII. - $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](images/95/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22ff9dcd-4fa1-5c0a-bb5b-2eec3be880ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-112591" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $x=-2$ is a simple root.\nII. - $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\nIII. - $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2832c750-5223-5055-a543-69f3bfc7bf6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48063" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5ba5f7cc2ea5aaf405db1abff7e6f28ed4378ba6a5ec88d73c6861ede1d75de.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ exists\nIII. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ breaks at $x=2$,  it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912ff428-058e-5197-af7a-fcbe7f3d6387" :question/math-academy-id "q-66785" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. - $x\\in (-\\infty ,3)$\nII. - $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\nIII. - $x\\in (-\\infty ,5]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-4|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-4|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-4|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-4|+2.$\n\n![](images/56/56956d0f23ae6828098507712fd00525dc4f114a195716dc16e63d8475e9c143.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,3).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (1,\\infty )$  and $(-\\infty ,5].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9857052-b60b-5b90-a22b-cd84e32dabbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-78133" :question/problem "![](images/43/43544a1ba403055094cae54fed9772df353d3697f50888728ebee61e77b729da.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-7$\nII. - $x=-4$\nIII. - $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-7.$ Because there is a jump at $x=-7,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-7$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a break at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cfd059-85bf-5a42-99f7-811485a4979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238504} {:answer/id #uuid "508f5c20-45d7-5202-90ab-5275c4e71dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238751} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1104d6-594d-5913-9b22-0cd4f65dd066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238844} {:answer/id #uuid "a55adbe4-dfc8-5852-b3c2-e09b91ff35b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239491} {:answer/id #uuid "fa180e81-a0f8-58ae-befc-5ded12bd0c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55adbe4-dfc8-5852-b3c2-e09b91ff35b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239491} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b7429d8-0653-5e56-8af7-5e36a2965088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2df95855-c5ea-57bc-9c0a-5ac2a69badf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104020" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c351342c55982f064955a311f9ea94220d58675e85c2e3514a12a8798f03df0.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a7b2e0-393c-52f9-9f69-6d2bc145fd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238084} {:answer/id #uuid "5a801021-53a0-5fe6-a9d2-ac3139f5e29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238837} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2fbbab-fc41-586d-bc31-f9711056f09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239987} {:answer/id #uuid "e03f563e-209c-5b0a-9b22-7def371eed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240016} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0cccfd-41bb-52ad-918d-e36b5148b3ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e03f563e-209c-5b0a-9b22-7def371eed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240016} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f7c316-c3c2-564d-8578-a6b33a465102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "597a789c-a68c-549f-b2b0-c1130bde6299" :question/math-academy-id "q-104023" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ee061e9348ada782791fca64a55923834dc87393518e9ec41c49698bfb2f92e.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d506aa-841e-5c5b-a1bf-5ac09f60017e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244603} {:answer/id #uuid "462c3d52-8b72-513b-980e-c15197879d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244940} {:answer/id #uuid "65c64b8c-1872-5671-805d-e70b0c6511d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245211} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1ce093-9dd6-5b3f-aef8-928fca896ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245542} {:answer/id #uuid "ede102f7-ea3c-59b6-a3e1-e4bb8edb3ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "462c3d52-8b72-513b-980e-c15197879d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244940} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83b8a24-f8de-5be9-ab19-5920066dd724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "163eeae3-71ad-5b66-a0aa-4b05d346c12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113525" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x({x}^{2}+x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+7)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+7=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(7) \\\\  & =1-28 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b06798-2160-5123-adeb-8fac4e25409f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244493} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb4901-52db-5ea7-b778-613db328f9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244797} {:answer/id #uuid "95e4ede5-330a-5d5d-b519-47ca590e8dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245627} {:answer/id #uuid "e1541557-1983-573e-b847-876677596fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246321} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4b0856-0eaf-556c-a63b-bf6bfda2eaae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1541557-1983-573e-b847-876677596fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246321} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce5440fc-dc76-5e79-91b4-ff690a389de3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daec1f65-9c08-5df4-939b-bebb2395a749" :question/math-academy-id "q-113545" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =1-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23779567-a8ad-53b9-bba7-20eb9434d446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260186} {:answer/id #uuid "252ff177-ca33-56d5-90df-65c900fa8710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260196} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0fd5bb-e524-591c-95eb-dd9ac92e0ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260410} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3682d9-c2f8-516c-9549-f226b96cd4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261080} {:answer/id #uuid "cfca039c-c8d9-5aa9-b3cf-495e5bcbfed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23779567-a8ad-53b9-bba7-20eb9434d446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260186} :answer-field/id #uuid "9694ecaf-94de-5d7f-a9c8-24cf2ab3c726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48261b12-c86c-55b1-9e2a-f38acbfa450a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128517" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0 \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3472f3af-6fdf-584a-814a-6ecb7e937778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263460} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe7a9e0-5fc1-5805-b935-6a06de4477cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263966} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f55d58-3424-5ef2-8a1f-da0ba8536bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264379} {:answer/id #uuid "ac46a045-085c-5912-a741-16ccb2b1102a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264480} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3d1d69-fac7-5c39-83ff-f1d420f126d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3472f3af-6fdf-584a-814a-6ecb7e937778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263460} :answer-field/id #uuid "8780a347-d9fa-54e7-9af1-9bb48bd7be2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50033102-58e0-5acc-81a2-81bf1717d471" :question/math-academy-id "q-130575" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & z \\\\ z & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis $-48$, find all the possible values of $z.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & z \\\\ z & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & z \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2 & z \\\\ z & 1\\end{aligned}|+5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ z & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(3+z)-(2-{z}^{2})+5(-2-3z) \\\\  & =6+2z-2+{z}^{2}-10-15z \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-13z-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-48,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-13z-6 & =-48 \\\\ {z}^{2}-13z+42 & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{-(-13)\\pm \\sqrt{{13}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 42}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pm \\sqrt{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pm 1}{2} \\\\  & =6,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=6$ and $z=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d38c9cc-ca7f-591e-8a8d-b51db0107fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263380} {:answer/id #uuid "e51b3361-87bb-58aa-8055-e13c58174671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264992} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c47758-325d-5cfc-acdb-c862f1f74e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265025} {:answer/id #uuid "ece5b351-94ef-5b84-a4b9-ea4263b1e26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265067} {:answer/id #uuid "f13d3fa2-d171-577a-a448-dd5b107fd44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ece5b351-94ef-5b84-a4b9-ea4263b1e26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265067} :answer-field/id #uuid "da945efe-22fe-5afe-9a1b-b80334eb4404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c642acf-da11-5fe2-a467-6e32aaff486e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130580" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$[\\begin{aligned}x & 2x & 3 \\\\ 5 & x & 7 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis $22$, find all the possible values of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}x & 2x & 3 \\\\ 5 & x & 7 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =x|\\begin{aligned}x & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2x|\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}5 & x \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x(x-14)-2x(5-21)+3(10-3x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-14x+32x+30-9x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9x+30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $22,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9x+30 & =22 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{{9}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 8}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm 7}{2} \\\\  & =-8,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-8$ and $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21390a7b-0a54-5e9a-9011-c7deff139c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263297} {:answer/id #uuid "91d75f72-1a96-5644-a529-caf7355ab1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264249} {:answer/id #uuid "970c8319-2067-5d80-a590-021621a09030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264292} {:answer/id #uuid "f080186a-d1c4-58fa-9d1f-db014dd38fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265109} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1c675c-a294-5ee2-a8ca-549d874e48c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-10$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91d75f72-1a96-5644-a529-caf7355ab1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264249} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69f31fe-6848-54a7-8e0e-0e1258ee7ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fee93468-4926-51f6-9486-b49c687c7f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130626" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $p$ given that the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3p & 4 \\\\ -2 & 6p & -8 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3p & 4 \\\\ -2 & 6p & -8 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}6p & -8 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-3p)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6p \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(6p-8)+3p(-2+8)+4(2-6p) \\\\  & =6p-8+18p+8-24p \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this matrix is singular for all values of $p.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04828dd4-f030-5633-b9ab-2ca90b202225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284827} {:answer/id #uuid "10872bea-02dc-53b8-8a81-d21f0443ee2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284932} {:answer/id #uuid "28dc2ce6-543c-571a-a921-bb628ebdeb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285145} {:answer/id #uuid "41f7dafc-fd39-5a57-8833-37de29245686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285398} {:answer/id #uuid "827cdff1-9d64-55e9-b43a-8f9a6a5b14e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41f7dafc-fd39-5a57-8833-37de29245686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285398} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf370fc0-22a1-5807-80d7-314f76c5d442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "627e7a3d-46e0-5ce8-b4f0-d14bd806cb23" :question/math-academy-id "q-18256" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of \n$[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{aligned}]$\n is $81,$ find all the possible values of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{aligned}| & =4|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ y & -5\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-3y & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-3y & -2 \\\\ 2 & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (10-y)-2\\cdot (15y-2)+3\\cdot (-3{y}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =40-4y-30y+4-9{y}^{2}+12 \\\\  & =-9{y}^{2}-34y+56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $81,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{y}^{2}-34y+56 & =81 \\\\ -9{y}^{2}-34y-25 & =0 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+34y+25 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{{34}^{2}-4\\cdot 9\\cdot 25}}{2\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{256}}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm 16}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-17\\pm 8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $y=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121a8609-4bb7-5c67-9bdc-2801df41173e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284949} {:answer/id #uuid "429a9de6-9e34-5d60-bd99-5ca64acccf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285403} {:answer/id #uuid "77aaca00-7a72-5562-b3c2-49acc2dc1a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285900} {:answer/id #uuid "a7439201-e9e4-57ad-a8e9-5b2518ed22ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286313} {:answer/id #uuid "c28c20d4-0f99-5907-b488-8276cbb024db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "429a9de6-9e34-5d60-bd99-5ca64acccf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ and $5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285403} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb400fb3-60c2-5a2f-a4e3-20a5f1ab4cc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a6ffa24-d6e8-52ac-ac3d-5fab4f24df3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-18272" :question/problem "Given that $M=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $det(M)=-23,$ find all the possible values of $p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}p & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-3)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ p-3 & -1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & p \\\\ p-3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (3p-{p}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3-2{p}^{2}+6p \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+6p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-23,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{p}^{2}+6p-3 & =-23 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}-6p-20 & =0 \\\\ {p}^{2}-3p-10 & =0 \\\\ p & =\\frac{-(-3)\\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-10)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm 7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions are $p=-2$ and $p=5.$"}]}
